+# Contract: Command-Line Interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-user-accounts-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-04
+
+This is the tool's whole external interface. It is the contract the tests assert
+against directly, by running the real executable in a subprocess with piped
+streams — not by calling Python functions.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH COMMAND USERNAME
+```
+
+- `src/` must be on `PYTHONPATH` (A-013); there is no install step
+  (Principle I).
+- `--store PATH` is **required**. `PATH` is the store file; the operator naming
+  it is the deliberate purpose of the invocation and it is not confined to any
+  base directory (SR-003).
+- `COMMAND` is exactly one of `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`,
+  `delete` (FR-001). Nothing else is accepted.
+- `USERNAME` is exactly one positional argument, 3–32 characters of
+  `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, compared case-sensitively (FR-011).
+- Option abbreviation is **not** accepted (`--sto` is an error), `--store=PATH`
+  is accepted as argparse's standard equivalent form, and there is no `-h` or
+  `--help`: every exit status must correspond to one of the six documented
+  conditions (FR-003), and help is not one of them.
+- No password may ever appear as an argument (FR-002).
+
+## Standard input protocol
+
+Passwords are read from stdin, one per line, in the order below (FR-002). A
+line is terminated by `\n`, optionally preceded by `\r`; the terminator is not
+part of the password (A-009). A final line at end-of-stream without a
+terminator is valid.
+
+| Command | Lines read | Order |
+|---|---|---|
+| `register` | 1 | new password |
+| `login` | 1 | password to verify |
+| `unlock` | **0** | — must work with stdin closed (A-006) |
+| `passwd` | 2 | current password, then new password |
+| `delete` | 1 | password to verify |
+
+Lines beyond those listed are never read and are not an error. Each line is at
+most 4 096 bytes before its terminator is stripped; a longer line is refused,
+not truncated (FR-014, SR-006). Each line must be valid UTF-8.
+
+## Success output
+
+On success a command writes only the text below to stdout, followed by a single
+newline, and writes **nothing** to stderr (FR-004).
+
+| Command | stdout | Exit |
+|---|---|---|
+| `register` | `registered USERNAME` | 0 |
+| `login` | `ok` | 0 |
+| `unlock` | *(nothing)* | 0 |
+| `passwd` | *(nothing)* | 0 |
+| `delete` | *(nothing)* | 0 |
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Exactly these six, and no others, for exactly these conditions (FR-003).
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | The operation completed |
+| 1 | Credentials rejected — wrong password, or an unknown account on `login` (A-001) |
+| 2 | Invalid request — bad usage, invalid username, password too short or too long when being set, over-long or non-UTF-8 input line, missing password input |
+| 3 | Account conflict — `register` on an existing username, or `unlock`/`passwd`/`delete` on an unknown username |
+| 4 | The account is locked |
+| 5 | The store could not be read, parsed or written |
+
+## Failure output
+
+On failure a command writes **one line** to stderr and **nothing** to stdout
+(FR-005). `invalid credentials` and `account locked` are exact, detail-free
+strings fixed by FR-005; every other message states what was expected and what
+was received. `USERNAME` below is echoed through a sanitiser that renders any
+character outside printable ASCII as `\xNN` and truncates at 64 characters
+(SR-002, SR-009). `N` is always a length or a count — never a password, salt,
+digest, parameter or any store content (SR-009).
+
+| Status | Message to stderr |
+|---|---|
+| 2 | `usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete} USERNAME` |
+| 2 | `error: unknown command 'COMMAND': expected one of register, login, unlock, passwd, delete` |
+| 2 | `error: invalid username 'USERNAME': expected 3-32 characters of letters, digits or underscore, received N characters` |
+| 2 | `error: invalid username 'USERNAME': expected only letters, digits or underscore` |
+| 2 | `error: new password too short: expected at least 12 characters, received N` |
+| 2 | `error: new password too long: expected at most 1024 characters, received N` |
+| 2 | `error: missing password input: expected N password line(s) on stdin, received M` |
+| 2 | `error: password line too long: expected at most 4096 bytes before the line terminator` |
+| 2 | `error: password line is not valid UTF-8 text` |
+| 1 | `invalid credentials` |
+| 4 | `account locked` |
+| 3 | `error: account 'USERNAME' already exists` |
+| 3 | `error: no such account 'USERNAME'` |
+| 5 | `error: cannot read store 'PATH': <errno description>` |
+| 5 | `error: cannot write store 'PATH': <errno description>` |
+| 5 | `error: store 'PATH' is malformed: <structural reason>` |
+| 5 | `error: store 'PATH' is too large: expected at most 8388608 bytes, received N` |
+| 5 | `error: store 'PATH' holds too many accounts: expected at most 10000, received N` |
+| 5 | `error: store 'PATH' is full: expected at most 10000 accounts, cannot add another` |
+
+The `<structural reason>` names the shape problem and never a value: e.g.
+`not a JSON object`, `unsupported version`, `record 'alice' has unexpected
+field`, `record 'alice' field 'failed_logins' out of range`, `nesting too
+deep`. The store path is echoed verbatim because SR-009 exempts the operator's
+own argument.
+
+## Indistinguishability guarantee
+
+A `login` against an account that does not exist and a `login` with a wrong
+password against an account that does exist produce the **identical** stderr
+line (`invalid credentials`), the identical exit status (1), identical empty
+stdout, and comparable wall-clock time — the unknown-account path performs one
+discarded scrypt derivation so the two cannot be told apart by timing (SC-004,
+SR-009).
+
+## Store side effects
+
+| Command | Creates the store? | Writes the store? |
+|---|---|---|
+| `register` | yes, on first use (FR-007) | on success only |
+| `login` | **never** (FR-007) | on success if the count was non-zero; on failure to increment the count |
+| `unlock` | **never** | on success only |
+| `passwd` | **never** | on success only |
+| `delete` | **never** | on success only |
+
+Against a store path that does not exist: `register` creates it; `login`
+returns 1; `unlock`, `passwd` and `delete` return 3 — and none of them leaves
+any file behind, not even a temporary or lock file (FR-007, SR-003).
+
+Every change becomes visible all at once (FR-009). Every failing command leaves
+the store byte-identical, with the single exception of the `login` failure-count
+increment (FR-006, SC-008).
+
+## Worked example sequence
+
+The spec's SC-011 refers to "the documented example sequence from the feature
+request", which the recorded request does not actually contain — see the
+clarification note in [plan.md](../plan.md#spec-gaps-flagged-not-blocking).
+This sequence stands as the documented one until that is resolved; it is
+asserted line for line, status for status.
+
+```console
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+invalid credentials                                   # stderr, exit 1 (no store, no account)
+$ ls ./s; echo "exit=$?"
+ls: cannot access './s': No such file or directory    # login created nothing
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s register alice
+registered alice                                      # stdout, exit 0
+$ stat -c '%a' ./s
+600                                                   # owner-only (SC-010)
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+ok                                                    # stdout, exit 0
+$ printf 'short\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s register bob
+error: new password too short: expected at least 12 characters, received 5
+                                                      # stderr, exit 2
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s register alice
+error: account 'alice' already exists                 # stderr, exit 3
+$ for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do printf 'wrong-password-here\n' \
+    | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice; echo "exit=$?"; done
+invalid credentials                                   # x5, exit 1 each
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+account locked                                        # stderr, exit 4 — correct password refused
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s unlock alice < /dev/null
+                                                      # no output, exit 0
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+ok                                                    # stdout, exit 0
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\nnew-password-here\n' \
+    | python -m accounts --store ./s passwd alice
+                                                      # no output, exit 0
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+invalid credentials                                   # old password dead, exit 1
+$ printf 'new-password-here\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s delete alice
+                                                      # no output, exit 0
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s unlock alice < /dev/null
+error: no such account 'alice'                        # stderr, exit 3
+```
